DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ffcclloossee() function dissociates the named _s_t_r_e_a_m from its underlying
     file or set of functions.  If the stream was being used for output, any
     buffered data is written first, using fflush(3).

     The ffddcclloossee() function is equivalent to ffcclloossee() except that it does not
     close the underlying file descriptor.  If _f_d_p is not NULL, the file de‐
     scriptor will be written to it.  If the _f_d_p argument will be different
     then NULL the file descriptor will be returned in it, If the stream does
     not have an associated file descriptor, _f_d_p will be set to -1.  This type
     of stream is created with functions such as fmemopen(3), funopen(3), or
     open_memstream(3).

     The ffcclloosseeaallll() function calls ffcclloossee() on all open streams.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
     ffcclloosseeaallll() does not return a value.

     Upon successful completion the ffcclloossee() and ffddcclloossee() functions return 0.
     Otherwise, EOF is returned and the global variable _e_r_r_n_o is set to indi‐
     cate the error.

EERRRROORRSS
     ffddcclloossee() fails if:

     [EOPNOTSUPP]       The stream does not have an associated file descrip‐
                        tor.

     The ffcclloossee() and ffddcclloossee() functions may also fail and set _e_r_r_n_o for any
     of the errors specified for fflush(3).

     The ffcclloossee() function may also fail and set errno for any of the errors
     specified for close(2).

NNOOTTEESS
     The ffcclloossee() and ffddcclloossee() functions do not handle NULL arguments in the
     _s_t_r_e_a_m variable; this will result in a segmentation violation.  This is
     intentional.  It makes it easier to make sure programs written under
     FreeBSD are bug free.  This behaviour is an implementation detail, and
     programs should not rely upon it.

